From: OnePlus kebab local bring-up <local@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: oneplus-kebab: use GIC MBI for PCIe2

The embedded SDX55 currently writes its MSI to the DesignWare internal
receiver address, but that transaction reaches the endpoint SMMU domain and
faults at IOVA 0x64100000. Route PCIe2 MSI through a 32-vector GICv3 MBI
range matching the stock interrupt allocation and doorbell alias.

This is a focused diagnostic change. It does not alter iommu-map or enable
any global IOMMU bypass.

---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
index 1e77d21ed..d3f6cc337 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
@@ -1318,3 +1318,22 @@ dai@78 {
 		reg = <TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3>;
 	};
 };
+
+/* kebab-sdx55-gic-mbi-test
+ *
+ * PCIe2 hosts the embedded SDX55 modem. Route its 32 MSI vectors through
+ * the GICv3 MBI path so the MSI doorbell is mapped into the endpoint IOMMU
+ * domain instead of using the DesignWare internal MSI receiver.
+ *
+ * OxygenOS uses GIC SPI 832..863 and a SETSPI doorbell at 0x17a10040.
+ * GIC MBI ranges use absolute INTIDs, so the matching range is 864..895.
+ */
+&intc {
+	msi-controller;
+	mbi-ranges = <864 32>;
+	mbi-alias = <0x0 0x17a10000>;
+};
+
+&pcie2 {
+	msi-parent = <&intc>;
+};
